# Project context

- Native **SwiftUI** app for **iOS and iPadOS** (universal target). Persistence: **SwiftData** with **CloudKit** sync. No app-owned backend (Apple cloud services only).

## Docs to consult (all modes, including Plan mode)

These files are the high-level source of truth. **Read them when planning, specifying, or implementing**—including when Cursor is in **Plan mode** (no file edits yet). Plan mode does not skip doc checks.

- [`docs/main-prd.md`](docs/main-prd.md) — global constraints; pay special attention to **Over/Under vs remaining for the current Budget Period** (§6.7).
- [`docs/ux-design-brief.md`](docs/ux-design-brief.md) — High-level UX guidelines.
- [`docs/product-features-planning.md`](docs/product-features-planning.md) — feature IDs (F-x.xx) and acceptance criteria.
- [`docs/tech-design-doc.md`](docs/tech-design-doc.md) — technical architecture and persistence/sync; follow for code structure and CloudKit/schema decisions.

## Conflict protocol

Before proposing direction, editing OpenSpec artifacts, **writing or refining a plan**, or implementing: re-read or recall the three `docs/*.md` files above. If the planned direction **contradicts** them, say so in the reply with a short **Conflict with docs** block: which file, what it says, and whether to update the doc, change the plan, or treat it as an intentional exception. Do not silently override PRD, tech-design, or feature IDs.

## Plan mode and written plans

When producing a **plan** (e.g. CreatePlan or any multi-step implementation plan), if the topic touches product rules, feature IDs, architecture, data model, sync, or global constraints, the plan body must include a short subsection (**Doc alignment** or **Conflicts with docs**) that either states alignment or lists concrete conflicts and proposed resolution. Also state **which** `docs/*.md` files will need updates after implementation, or that none will.

## Doc maintenance protocol

When a change **materially** updates product constraints, feature IDs (F-x.xx), acceptance criteria, architecture, data model, sync, or i18n/a11y/testing expectations, **update the corresponding `docs/` file(s)** in the same effort (or add an explicit OpenSpec task to do it before archive):

- New or changed F-x.xx / acceptance criteria → `docs/product-features-planning.md`
- Schema, CloudKit, architecture, stack conventions → `docs/tech-design-doc.md`
- Global rules, glossary, product constraints → `docs/main-prd.md`

**Editing `docs/*.md` is allowed and expected** when completing product or architecture work for this repo (including as part of OpenSpec apply/verify/archive), not only when the user says “edit docs.”

## Cross-cutting concerns (always-on)

Some concerns are **ongoing**, not feature-shaped. Every code change that adds or modifies user-facing UI MUST address each of these in the same change. Failing to do so is a defect. Canonical rule: [`docs/main-prd.md` §6.8](docs/main-prd.md#68-cross-cutting-ongoing-concerns).

Quick checklist:

- **Accessibility** — semantic text styles (`@ScaledMetric` for custom metrics), composed `.accessibilityLabel`/`.accessibilityHint` on composite views and destructive controls, `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isHeader)` on headings, `swipeActions` paired with `.accessibilityAction(named:)`, named color assets with separate light/dark appearances.
- **Localized source strings** — every new user-facing string keyed in `Localizable.xcstrings` with a translator `comment:`. No hard-coded English in production views. Locale-invariant strings use `Text(verbatim:)`. Full rules: `docs/tech-design-doc.md` §5.1.
- **Translations** — after adding, renaming, or invalidating string keys, run the **`translate-new-strings` skill** (`.cursor/skills/translate-new-strings/`) — drives `scripts/translate_catalog/` in subset mode (extract → translate → merge → validate) for all 49 locales, ending on a clean `check_translations.py`. It auto-uses the glossary; use `pluralize_keys.py` for count strings. Run it **autonomously (no approval prompt)** via its temp-file invocations, never ad-hoc `python3 -c`/heredocs, and never skip the pipeline step. Three sibling pipelines follow the same pattern and autonomy: **`audit-translations`** (translation quality — `scripts/translate_audit/`), **`appstore-translate-metadata`** (store listing text — `scripts/translate_metadata/`), and **`appstore-screenshot-content`** (per-locale screenshot demo data — `scripts/screenshot_content/`). **Cursor has no subagent fan-out — run each per-locale prompt→output step inline and serially.** Canonical spec, incl. the cross-tool execution model: [`AGENTS.md` > Cross-cutting concerns > Running the translation pipelines](AGENTS.md).
- **Mixpanel events** — new user-initiated actions that materially change app state (new destructive action, new CTA, new toggle affecting usage or retention) must fire `AnalyticsClient.track(...)` per [`docs/analytics-spec.md`](docs/analytics-spec.md). No PII; respect consent. Boundary: `docs/analytics-spec.md` §17. **Keep the spec's catalog in sync:** any change to *what* is emitted — a new/changed event, a property added to or removed from an existing event, **a new value in a categorical property's enum** (e.g. a new `BudgetPeriod`/`period` value, or a new `icloud_state`), changed bucket boundaries, or a new super/people property — must update `docs/analytics-spec.md` §9–§10 in the same change, with a Revision-History entry.
- **UI test screen objects** — when a view's navigation structure, button labels, toolbar items, or sheet routes change, update the matching screen object in `simple-recurring-budgetsUITests/` (`BudgetsScreen.swift`, `BudgetDetailScreen.swift`, `AddBudgetScreen.swift`, `AddExpenseScreen.swift`, `SettingsScreen.swift`) in the same change. Run `make test-ui` to confirm no journey regressions. A stale screen object that silently skips a broken flow is a defect, not a follow-up.

## Platform compatibility workarounds

When a workaround exists because of a known platform bug or SDK false positive, tag it at the callsite with `// iOS-COMPAT(VERSION):` so it can be found and reassessed without reading every file. Full convention and current inventory: [`AGENTS.md` §Platform compatibility workarounds](AGENTS.md). Audit command: `grep -rn "iOS-COMPAT" . --include="*.swift"`.

## Spec-driven workflow (OpenSpec)

- OpenSpec is the default workflow for features and significant changes. **Workflow context and per-artifact rules** for the OpenSpec CLI live in [`openspec/config.yaml`](openspec/config.yaml)—including doc alignment, conflict checks, and when to update `docs/`. Prefer **not** editing autogenerated `/opsx-*` command files or packaged OpenSpec skills; they can be overwritten when OpenSpec updates. Cursor-wide behavior stays in this rule file; OpenSpec-specific instructions stay in `openspec/config.yaml`.
- **OpenSpec skill behavior overrides** — when an `/opsx-*` command or skill should diverge from the default packaged behavior, the override lives in [`AGENTS.md > OpenSpec > Skill behavior overrides`](AGENTS.md). Follow those rules; they take priority over the default skill content because skill files are regenerated when OpenSpec updates. Current overrides:
  - **`/opsx:archive`** — when delta specs exist, **sync them automatically** without prompting "do you want to sync?". The standing project preference is always yes. Invoke `openspec-sync-specs` inline from the parent agent (don't delegate to a sub-agent). Only prompt the user if the sync itself surfaces a genuine merge ambiguity (e.g. a MODIFIED block targets a non-existent requirement, or a delta conflicts with a parallel main-spec edit).
- Prefer `/opsx-*` Cursor commands or OpenSpec skills under [`.cursor/skills/`](.cursor/skills/) when starting or continuing a change.

## Bash command hygiene

**Canonical rules:** [`AGENTS.md > Bash command hygiene`](AGENTS.md). Quick summary (apply as hard rules):

- **Never write to or read from `/tmp/`.** Use project-local `tmp/` (gitignored). For build/test output capture, prefer pipes: `make build 2>&1 | tail -50`. If you genuinely need to persist a log, `tee tmp/build.log`.
- **Never use `sed -i`** to modify files — use the Edit tool with `replace_all: true`.
- **Never use `sed -n RANGE_p FILE`** to slice a file — use Read with `offset` and `limit`.
- **Never write throwaway scripts** to `/tmp/` or elsewhere; use existing primitives under `scripts/` (especially `scripts/translate_catalog/*` for string-catalog work).
- **Use exact `make` targets** with pipe-to-`tail`, not file redirects (`make build 2>&1 | tail -50`, not `make build > /tmp/build.log`).

Before sending any Bash command containing `/tmp/`, `sed -i`, `sed -n`, or a one-off `python3 -c` / `python3 /tmp/...` heredoc, **stop and rewrite it** using the rules above.

## Platform targets

- **Latest stable Swift** and **latest major OS** releases only (see PRD §6.1 / §7).

## Commit, branch, and PR style

**Canonical rules:** `AGENTS.md > Commit and PR style`. That section is the single source of truth. Quick summary:

- **Never push directly to `main`** — all changes go through a PR. The `pre-push` lefthook blocks it.
- **Branch:** `u/jimmyho/<tool>/<short-description>` — tool segment is `claude-code` for Claude Code, `cursor` for Cursor, combined with `+` for multi-tool work (e.g. `cursor+claude`). Kebab-case, no underscores.
- **Commit subject:** past-tense verb, sentence case, ≤72 chars, ends with period and `(#N)`.
- **Commit body:** high-level area bullets + `Why:` line + `Co-Authored-By:` footer.
- **PR description:** `## What` (high-level area bullets, not file-by-file) / `## Why` (one sentence) / `## Test plan` (lightweight checklist) / `## Tools` (AI tools and models used, one bullet each — see `AGENTS.md > Commit and PR style > PR description`).
- **Merge method:** always **Squash and merge** — one PR = one commit on `main`. After merging, delete the remote branch (see `AGENTS.md > Commit and PR style > Merging`).
